CSS Optimization: Is Using Declarations Just Once (UDJO) Still Effective Today?

Published on Oct 13, 2026, filed under development , css , maintainability , performance , optimization . ( Share this post , e.g., on Mastodon .) CSS optimization is a deceptive topic. On the one hand, it feels like something simple that we should already have all the tooling for—especially when it comes to minification. On the other hand, the idea that we already exploited “everything” there is around CSS optimization can be considered naive. Such a claim looks hard to defend for a language that evolves almost daily. In this article, I’d like to pick a technique that’s at least 18 years old —“UDJO,” using declarations just once. (Aware of an older source? Please let me know !) The technique is inspired by the principle of DRY, “don’t repeat yourself,” in that it works by identifying and reducing (if not eliminating) declaration repetition. So if there’s something like display: flex , and it appears two or more times, it gets reconciled in one rule: .example {

} The outcome of the technique depends—on how intelligently the deduplication is done and how long the selectors are. Intelligence determines if UDJO leads to an actual maintainability advantage—can the change of a declaration really be done to all the selectors attached?—, and the selector length influences how big file size savings are. If you're interested, DRY CSS describes the mechanics in more detail. The question here is: Is UDJO still effective and still relevant today? Better Deduplication Through Tooling

When we try to answer this question, we end up with a problem: When sites are complex and their style sheet declarations have not been de-duplicated before, UDJO requires a lot of work. To save some time, I took this to build tooling for this work. While it didn’t seem like it would immediately be as comprehensive as manually going through and testing style sheets, it seemed realistic enough that a tool could catch some unnecessary repetition. Skipping ahead, I created CSS Dedup —not only to identify repetition, but also to deduplicate what could safely be optimized (CSS Dedup has some documentation on this but in essence, deduplication affects selector locations, which, as per the cascade , influences what rules are being applied). This, then, gives us some data: Is There Anything to Deduplicate in Modern CSS?

For a first batch, I picked 20 “random” websites to analyze. I then used CSS Dedup to check what the file size changes would be if we deduplicated what seemed provably safe, and also what seemed probably safe, but only if it led to file size savings. I picked those two options to provide the “natural” result of deduplication, but also the one site owners may be most interested in to reduce repetition but also decrease transfer size. After all, UDJO as a CSS optimization technique can improve both maintainability and performance. Here are the findings: Site UDJO Effect (External Style Sheets) ( -f ) Aggressive With Savings Only ( -f -a -s ) 12371.cn +2.9 KB (+14.3%) -45 B (-0.2%) ccc.de +0.8 KB (+2.2%) -0.1 KB (-0.3%) commission.europa.eu +64.9 KB (+6.4%) -51.9 KB (-5.1%) die-linke.de +104.0 KB (+20.5%) -21.2 KB (-4.2%) diem25.org +40.5 KB (+10.7%) -4.4 KB (-1.2%) eff.org +120.8 KB (+27.7%) -8.5 KB (-2.0%) en.mfa.ir +58.7 KB (+5.1%) -51.2 KB (-4.5%) euroveg.eu +78.8 KB (+9.3%) -12.4 KB (-1.5%) globalsumudflotilla.org -17.0 KB (-6.5%) -18.7 KB (-7.2%) gruene.de +1.6 KB (+1.1%) -0.7 KB (-0.5%) icanw.org +5.5 KB (+2.9%) -3.5 KB (-1.8%) laquadrature.net +14.0 KB (+4.2%) -3.0 KB (-0.9%) middleeasteye.net +60.9 KB (+8.6%) -20.5 KB (-2.9%) movimientosumar.es +98.8 KB (+13.6%) -5.0 KB (-0.7%) nam.go.ug +84.5 KB (+9.2%) -53.9 KB (-5.9%) oic-oci.org -6.3 KB (-4.0%) -9.3 KB (-6.0%) podemos.info +23.2 KB (+5.5%) -6.0 KB (-1.4%) taxmenow.eu +1.7 KB (+2.1%) -4.0 KB (-4.6%) unrwa.org +87.4 KB (+13.2%) -4.8 KB (-0.7%) veganeurope.eu +37.3 KB (+17.7%) -1.3 KB (-0.6%) Important note:…
